>
  <p>
    Almost everyone has played solitaire, yet few people know where it came from. The game most of
    us picture — a fan of cards, four foundations, a quiet few minutes alone — is the product of
    two very different histories: a couple of centuries of European card play, and a few decades of
    computing that turned a niche pastime into one of the most-played games on Earth. This is the
    short version of how that happened.
  </p>

  <h2>Origins: the age of "patience"</h2>
  <p>
    Single-player card games — games you play alone, against the luck of the deal — appear to have
    emerged in Europe by the late 18th to early 19th century, with northern Europe, France and
    Germany among the regions most often associated with their early spread. The British settled on
    the word <strong>patience</strong>, which nicely describes the unhurried, one-step-at-a-time
    rhythm of the play; American English adopted <strong>solitaire</strong>, from the French for
    "solitary." Both names describe the same idea: one player, one deck, no opponent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The games were passed around informally at first, but by the 1800s printed compilations of
    patience games began to appear, gathering dozens of variants and their rules between two covers.
    These early collections helped standardise games that had previously drifted from household to
    household, and they hint at just how popular solo card play had already become well before any
    machine could deal a hand for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the names came from</h2>
  <p>
    The variant most people now simply call "Solitaire" is <strong>Klondike</strong>, and its name
    is tied to the <strong>Klondike Gold Rush</strong> of the 1890s in North America — the era of
    prospectors heading north in search of fortune. Exactly who first attached the name to the game
    is hard to pin down, but the association with that frontier moment stuck, and Klondike became
    the default face of solitaire for generations to come.
  </p>

  <h2>FreeCell and the computer age</h2>
  <p>
    Solitaire's leap from the card table to the screen began earlier than most people assume. A
    playable computer version of <a href="/freecell/"><strong>FreeCell</strong></a> was written by
    <strong>Paul Alfille in 1978</strong> on the PLATO system, an early networked educational
    computing platform. FreeCell is unusual among patience games: nearly every deal is solvable, so
    success leans heavily on skill rather than luck — a quality that made it a natural fit for
    thoughtful play.
  </p>
  <p>
    FreeCell became famous, though, when <strong>Microsoft</strong> bundled it with Windows in the
    Win32s / Windows 95 era. That version shipped with a now-legendary set of
    <strong>32,000 numbered deals</strong>, which gave players a shared frame of reference and a way
    to compare notes on tricky hands. (One of those numbered games became notorious for being
    extraordinarily hard to beat.) Numbered, reproducible deals are an idea that survives today — it
    is exactly what lets a modern site let you replay or share an identical hand.
  </p>

  <h2>The cultural turning point: Windows Solitaire</h2>
  <p>
    If FreeCell brought solitaire to computing, <strong>Windows Solitaire</strong> brought it to
    everyone. Microsoft included Klondike "Solitaire" with <strong>Windows 3.0 in 1990</strong>,
    putting the game in front of a vast new audience of office workers and home users practically
    overnight. According to Microsoft lore, the game was not just a giveaway: it was meant to teach
    a generation still uneasy with the mouse how to <strong>drag and drop</strong> — sliding a card
    onto a pile is the gentlest possible lesson in a gesture the whole graphical interface relied on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whatever the intent, the result was extraordinary. Solitaire quickly became one of the
    most-played computer games in the world, a fixture of coffee breaks and quiet afternoons, and
    for many people their very first experience of "gaming" on a PC at all.
  </p>

  <h2>The web and mobile era</h2>
  <p>
    From there, solitaire followed its players everywhere. It moved onto the open web and, later,
    onto the phone in your pocket, where it remains a daily habit for millions — a few hands while
    the kettle boils or the bus arrives. Modern versions add conveniences the card table never had:
    unlimited <strong>undo</strong>, on-demand <strong>hints</strong>, running
    <strong>statistics</strong>, and <a href="/daily-challenge/">daily challenges</a> that give a
    shared puzzle to everyone at once. CardHearth sits squarely in this tradition — the same old
    games, with the small modern touches that make them easier to learn and pleasant to return to.
  </p>

